            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            (although we are somewhat careless with the language and do not always point out when a set may predicatively be a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
            %a{:href => "http://www.paultaylor.eu/ASD/dedras/intro"} Bauer &amp; Taylor
            (although so far we cover only the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heine%E2%80%93Borel+theorem"} Heine–Borel theorem
          %p
            The exposition here is pretty much
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toby+Bartels"} Toby Bartels
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#zigzaglemma"}
            is Bartels's as far as they know (but it's not very deep, just bookkeeping). The version of
            %span.newWikiWord
              Cousin's Theorem
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Cousin%27s+theorem"} ?
            that appears here may also be original.
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            To describe the locale of the real numbers, we need first of all to describe what an
            %em open
            (in the axiomatic sense, equivalent to an
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sublocale"} open sublocale
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+intervals"} open intervals
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            that it contains. This is analogous to the key insight of
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Dedekind"} Richard Dedekind
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "real+number#Dedekind"} definition of real number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            that it belongs to. (Once one is talking about rational numbers, things are manageable.)
          %h2#opens Opens
          %p
            An
            %strong open
            in the real line is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relation"} binary relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\sim}
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            that satisfies the four properties listed below. Intuitively, we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim b
            iff the open
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b)
            is contained in the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subspace"} open set
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                If
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ≥
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \geq b
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim b
            %li
              %p
                If
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ≥
                        %mi b
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi c
                        %mo ≥
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \geq b \sim c \geq d
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim d
            %li
              %p
                If
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi b
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mi c
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim b \gt c \sim d
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim d
            %li
