          %h2#the_zigzag_lemma The Zigzag Lemma
          %p A zigzag may not look like the picture above; instead of an orderly progression, the zigs and zags may be wild swings that are undone by other zags and zigs. It is sometimes convenient to replace a zigzag by a more orderly one. Of course, this is easy if all of the zigs belong to a single open, since (by property (3) of the definition of an open), the whole zigzag can be replaced by a single zig. But we'll also want to consider zigzags where the zigs are taken from different opens. The following definitions make precise what kind of zigzag we'll want, and then there will be a lemma that we can indeed have this.
          #zigzagdefn.num_defn
            %h6#definitions Definitions
            %p
              Given rational numbers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              and a positive natural number
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "-tuple" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
